To verify if this S3 bucket is public or misconfigured, you can test it using the AWS CLI tool we just installed. Bug bounty platforms generally require a Proof of Concept (PoC) demonstrating listing, reading, or writing capabilities before accepting a submission.
Here are the standard, non-destructive commands to test the permissions safely:
## 1. Test for Public Listing
Check if anyone on the internet can list the contents of the bucket without using any AWS credentials:

aws s3 ls s3://web-k8s-forum-prod --no-sign-request --region eu-central-1

## 2. Test for Authenticated Listing (Any AWS Account)
Sometimes buckets are misconfigured to allow access to "Any Authenticated AWS User" (which means anyone with a valid AWS account, not just users inside the target organization). You can test this using your own local AWS credentials (after running aws configure):

aws s3 ls s3://web-k8s-forum-prod --region eu-central-1

